rom ep-exdos13.rom page 0 cpu z80
default observed

# Range 7: the end of DISK_IO and its head-command waits, then FLOPPY_DRIVER's functions
# ($1FD3): 0/1 initialise, 2 media check, 3 log the disk in (boot sector, BPB), 4-6 read, write
# and write with verify, 8 format; the transfer with its retries and the double-step check.
#
# The driver keeps one byte per drive (n = 0-3) in arrays addressed as IX = IY + n:
# IX-$0B the select bit, IX-$07 flags (bit 0 step checked, 1 double step, 2 over 50 tracks,
# 3 parameters known), IX-$03 sectors per track, IX+$01 the last track, IX+$05 sides, IX+$09 and
# IX+$0D the total sectors (low, high), IX+$11 and IX+$15 the first sector's offset, IX+$19 the
# media byte. IY-$12 holds the function, IY-$11 to IY-$0C an address field read by ADDR_READ.

@1EFA DIO_STATUS
  A sector command ended: the status's error bits ($5C) or an earlier result in D end the
  operation; else, while sectors are left in B, the sector register is advanced and the next
  sector's command is issued (DIO_OP_CMD) with the flags at IY-$16 = $80.
  .1EFA The command/status register
  .1EFC The status
  .1EFE Write protect, not found, CRC or lost data?
  .1F00 Yes: the result
  .1F02 An earlier result (time-out, mismatch)?
  .1F05 One sector fewer: none left?
  .1F08 The sector register
  .1F0A The sector just done
  .1F0D The next sector
  .1F0F Back to the command register
  .1F11 Flags: motor on, no head move, same drive
  .1F15 D = the operation
  .1F18 Its command for the next sector

@1F1B DIO_ST_RESULT
  The result is the status's error bits (0 when the count ran out).
  .1F1B D = the result

@1F1C DIO_FINISH
  After a write (2) or a track write (4), a short wait of 256 DEC/JR passes before the end.
  .1F1C A = the operation
  .1F1F Write?
  .1F23 Write track?
  .1F25 No: no wait

@1F27 DIO_WR_SETTLE
  The 256-pass wait after a write.

@1F2A DIO_INT_RESTORE
  Sets the count at IY-$5F to $6E and enables interrupts again unless INT_DISABLE (called at
  $1E27) found them disabled.
  .1F2A The count at IY-$5F
  .1F2E The state kept at $1E2A: C if they were off
  .1F2F Were off: leave them
  .1F31 Were on

@1F32 DIO_DONE_COUNT
  L = the sectors done (the count asked for less those left in B); stores R in the next of the
  4 bytes at IY-$4A (IY-$1F counts the stores) and leaves through DIO_EXIT with A = the result.
  .1F32 HL = the caller's HL (L = the sector count)
  .1F34 Less the sectors not done
  .1F35 L = the sectors done
  .1F36 A = the store count
  .1F39 Counted
  .1F3C 0-3: which byte
  .1F41 IX = IY + that
  .1F47 A = R
  .1F49 Into one of IY-$4A to IY-$47
  .1F4C A = the result
  .1F4D Puts back BC, IX, DE; flags from A

@1F58 FDC_WAIT_COUNT
  Part of FDC_CMD_FLAGS: the wait count is $9331 while the motor runs, else 0 (65,536).
  .1F58 The wait while the motor runs
  .1F5B Motor running (flags bit 7)?
  .1F60 No: the longest wait

@1F8B FDC_HEAD_WAIT
  Part of FDC_TYPE1_CMD: a new drive was selected, so waits $2C x 256 passes for the head to
  load.
  .1F8E $2C rounds

@1F90 FDC_MOTOR_CHK
  When the motor was off, marks it running and waits for it: up to $E935 passes, cut short once
  index pulses (the latch's bit 0) come in.
  .1F90 Motor already running?
  .1F92 Yes: no wait
  .1F94 Marks it running
  .1F96 The drive latch
  .1F98 B = the latch now
  .1F9A The longest wait

@1F9D FDC_INDEX_WAIT
  Each change of the index bit cuts the passes left to at most $0CFF.
  .1F9D The latch
  .1F9F Index bit changed?
  .1FA1 No
  .1FA3 B = the latch now
  .1FA5 More than $0CFF passes left?
  .1FA8 No: count on
  .1FAA Yes: $0Cxx

@1FAB FDC_INDEX_NEXT
  Counts the motor wait down.
  .1FAE Not run out: watch the index bit

@1FB0 FDC_SAVE_FLAGS
  Stores the flags in E at IY-$16. Operations 1 and 3 (read, verify) enter at $1FB1, inside the
  LD HL, which is RES 2,E: they skip the waits and clear the head-moved mark, so their command
  does not wait for the head to settle.
  .1FB0 Hides RES 2,E at $1FB1, the entry for reads and verifies
  .1FB3 The flags

@1FB6 FDC_BUSY_WAIT
  Waits while the controller is busy (the count at IY-$5F held at $6E), then returns A = 2 when
  the status shows track 0, else 0.
  .1FB6 The count at IY-$5F
  .1FBA The command/status register
  .1FBC The status
  .1FBE Busy?
  .1FC1 The caller's HL
  .1FC2 A = 2 at track 0 (status bit 2)

@1FEB FD_INIT
  FLOPPY_DRIVER functions 0 and 1: forgets every drive's track (DISK_IO 0, B = $0F), asks drives
  3 to 0 for a disk change (DISK_IO 7), which takes a pending change off the latch, and stores
  the select bits 1, 2, 4, 8 at IY-$0B to IY-$08. Function 1 then returns A = 0; function 0
  restores drive 0, and drive 1 when drive 0 is at track 0 (DISK_IO 8 with B = 0, step rate 0).
  IY is 4 higher when it leaves (the select-bit loop steps it); DRIVER_CALL gives the driver its
  IY afresh on every call.
  .1FEB A' = function - 2: $FE for 0, $FF for 1
  .1FED All four drives
  .1FEF Forgets their tracks
  .1FF2 Drive 3 first

@1FF4 FD_INIT_CHANGE
  One drive per pass, 3 down to 0.
  .1FF4 B = the drive's bit
  .1FF5 Disk change report
  .1FFC Not past drive 0: the next one
  .1FFE 4 drives; drive 0's bit first

@2001 FD_INIT_BITS
  Stores each drive's select bit, then ends the function.
  .2001 The drive's select bit
  .2004 The next drive's bit
  .2006 The next drive's slot
  .200A A = function - 2
  .200B Function 1: done, A = 0
  .200D Count tracks; B = 0: drives 0 and 1
  .200F H = 0: step rate 0
  .2010 Function 0 returns DISK_IO's result

@22C1 FD_UNIT_SLOT
  Every function's start: checks the unit's drive number in C and points IX at the drive's slot
  in the per-drive arrays (IY + the drive), keeping the caller's IX (the buffer) in HL'.
  A drive number of 4 or more leaves the driver function at once with error $BC (FD_UNIT_BAD).
  Entry: C = the drive (0-3); B = the caller's value (a count or a media byte); IX = the buffer;
  IY = the driver's variables.
  Exit: IX = IY + C; A = the entry B; B = the drive's select bit; HL' = the entry IX; DE' = C.
  Error: drops its return address and returns from the driver function with A = $BC, B = 0.
  Changes: AF, B, IX, DE', HL'.
  Keeps: C, DE, HL, IY.
  .22C1 A = the drive
  .22C2 Drive 0-3?
  .22C4 No: error $BC
  .22C7 DE' = the drive
  .22CA IX = IY, the caller's IX on the stack
  .22CE IX = the drive's slot
  .22D0 HL' = the caller's IX (the buffer)
  .22D2 A = the caller's B
  .22D3 B = the drive's select bit

@22D7 FD_UNIT_BAD
  No such drive: returns from the driver function with A = $BC (a code with no message) and
  B = 0.
  .22D7 Drops the return into the function
  .22D8 Error $BC
  .22DA Nothing done

@2013 FD_MEDIA_CHECK
  FLOPPY_DRIVER function 2: has the disk in the drive changed? B = $FF when the drive's
  parameters are not known or DISK_IO 7 reports a new change (or fails); B = 0 when the drive
  has no change mark; with a change mark B = 1 when IY-$45 is not 0, else 0. A = 0 every time.
  .2013 IX = the drive's slot, B = its select bit
  .2016 Parameters known?
  .201A No: changed
  .201C Disk change report
  .2021 A new change (or a failure)
  .2023 B = the drive's change mark
  .2025 None: B = 0
  .2027 IY-$45 = 0?
  .202A B = 0
  .202B Yes: B = 0
  .202C No: B = 1

@202E FD_MEDIA_UNKNOWN
  B = $FF: the disk may have changed; A = 0.
  .202E Changed or not known

@2032 FD_LOG_DISK
  FLOPPY_DRIVER function 3: reads the disk's layout and sets the drive's parameters.
  1. Marks the parameters not known, forgets the track and reads track 0 sector 1 into the
     caller's buffer (FD_XFER_RETRY).
  2. Takes the BPB at +$0B, or the record at +$50 (BOOT_BPB_FIND); failing both, reads sector 2
     and builds the BPB from the media byte that starts the FAT (FD_TRY_FAT_ID).
  3. Stores the geometry in the drive's slot (FD_SET_PARAMS), checking side 1 of a two-sided
     disk, and the double step for a new disk of more than 50 tracks.
  .2032 IX = the drive's slot, B = its select bit, HL' = buffer
  .2035 Parameters not known
  .2039 DISK_IO 0
  .203A Forgets the drive's track
  .203D Track 0, sector 1
  .2040 Side 0
  .2041 One sector
  .2043 Keeps the buffer
  .2045 Reads it
  .2048 HL = the buffer
  .2049 Failed
  .204D A usable BPB in it?
  .2051 No: try the FAT's media byte
  .2053 Drops the buffer
  .2054 Z: the drive's parameters from it

@2057 FD_TRY_FAT_ID
  No usable BPB in the boot sector: reads sector 2 (the FAT's first sector) into the buffer; a
  media byte of $F0 or more followed by $FF, $FF gives a BPB built from it (BPB_FROM_MEDIA), else
  error $B4.
  .2057 HL = the buffer
  .205A HL' = the buffer
  .205D Track 0, sector 2
  .2060 Side 0
  .2061 One sector
  .2063 Reads it
  .2066 HL = the buffer
  .2067 Failed
  .2069 C = the first byte (the media byte)
  .206D The next two bytes
  .206E Both $FF?
  .206F No: not a DOS disk
  .2072 Media byte $F0 or more?
  .2074 No: not a DOS disk
  .2076 HL = the buffer
  .2078 The BPB at +$0B from the media byte
  .207B NC: the drive's parameters from it

@207E FD_BOOT_RD_ERR
  The boot sector could not be read. A time-out or a disk change is mapped to its error; else
  an address field is read on the track: if one is found the disk is formatted ($B4 Not a DOS
  disk); if not, a second try with the latch's bit 5 set decides between $B4 and $B5.
  .207E Time-out or disk change?
  .2080 Yes: map it
  .2082 Reads an address field here
  .2085 Time-out or disk change?
  .2089 Record not found?
  .208B No: a formatted disk, not DOS
  .208D Stay on the track; bit 5 to the latch
  .2090 Tries again

@2093 FD_FAT_RD_ERR
  A read of sector 2 failed (D = 0 here: $B4 unless a time-out or change), or the second
  address-field read ended (D = its result).
  .2093 Time-out or disk change?

@2095 FD_UNFORMATTED
  Maps a time-out or a disk change; else a record not found gives $B5 (Unformatted disk) and
  anything else $B4.
  .2095 Yes: map it
  .2098 Record not found?
  .209A Error $B5 (Unformatted disk)

@209D FD_NOT_DOS
  Error $B4 (Not a DOS disk).
  .209D Error $B4

@20E0 BPB_CHECK
  Checks a BPB: 512 bytes per sector, a power of 2 sectors per cluster, 1-7 FATs, 1-12 sectors
  per FAT.
  Entry: HL = the BPB (the bytes-per-sector word, boot sector +$0B).
  Exit: Z when it passes: A = the sectors per track's low byte, HL = BPB+$0E (its high byte).
  Error: NZ when a field is out of range (BPB_BAD makes it NZ with OR H).
  Changes: AF, DE, HL.
  Keeps: BC, IX, IY.
  .20E0 D = bytes per sector, low
  .20E2 A = high
  .20E4 $0200?
  .20E7 No: refused
  .20E8 Sectors per cluster
  .20E9 0: refused
  .20EB A power of 2?
  .20F0 No: refused
  .20F1 BPB+$05: the number of FATs
  .20F4 1-7?
  .20F7 No: refused
  .20F9 DE = 6 (D = 0 from the check)
  .20FB BPB+$0B: sectors per FAT
  .20FE 1-12?
  .2101 No: refused
  .2103 Its high byte
  .2105 Z when 0
  .2106 A = sectors per track, low

@20DE BPB_BAD
  A refused BPB: NZ (A OR H, H being the buffer's high byte).
  .20DE NZ: refused

@20A0 BOOT_BPB_FIND
  Looks for a usable BPB in the boot sector: the one at +$0B (BPB_CHECK, then 1-128 sectors per
  track and 1 or 2 sides), else the record at +$50 when byte +8 is 1 (BOOT_ALT_BPB).
  Entry: HL = the boot sector.
  Exit: Z. NC with HL = the boot sector +$0B; or C with HL = the boot sector +$50, its 15 bytes
  copied over +$0B to +$19 and A = its +$0D byte (taken as the sides).
  Error: NZ when neither passes.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL.
  Keeps: IX, IY.
  .20A0 The BPB at +$0B
  .20A4 Kept
  .20A5 Passes the first checks?
  .20A8 No: try the record at +$50
  .20AA Sectors per track 1-128?
  .20AF D = its high byte
  .20B1 A = the sides' low byte
  .20B3 1 or 2 sides: 0
  .20B6 With the high bytes
  .20B8 Not 0: try the record at +$50
  .20BA HL = the BPB

@20BC BOOT_ALT_BPB
  The BPB at +$0B failed: when the boot sector's byte +8 is 1, checks the record at +$50 the
  same way (with its +$0D byte below 3) and copies its 15 bytes over the BPB at +$0B.
  .20BC HL = the boot sector +$0B
  .20BF HL = +$08
  .20C0 Byte +8 = 1?
  .20C2 No: NZ, refused
  .20C3 The record at +$50
  .20C7 Passes the checks?
  .20CB Its +$0D byte (sides) below 3?
  .20CD No: refused
  .20D0 DE = the boot sector +$19
  .20D5 15 bytes, +$5E down to +$50
  .20D8 Over +$19 down to +$0B
  .20DA HL = the record at +$50
  .20DB Z
  .20DC C: the record at +$50 was taken

@2109 BPB_FROM_MEDIA
  Builds a BPB at +$0B of the buffer from a FAT media byte (bit 0: two sides, bit 1: 8 sectors
  per track, bit 2: 40 tracks): 512 bytes per sector; 1 sector per cluster when the bits 0 and 2
  give 4 ($FC, $FE), else 2; 1 reserved sector; 2 FATs; $40 root entries (with 1 per cluster)
  or $70; 320 or 360 sectors, doubled for two sides and again for 80 tracks; 1 sector per FAT
  for $FA, $FE, $FF, 3 for $F9, else 2. Bytes +$0B to +$63 are cleared first.
  Entry: A = C = the media byte; HL = the buffer.
  Exit: HL = the buffer +$0B; NC.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL.
  Keeps: IX, IY.
  .2109 D = 0
  .210C HL = the buffer +$64
  .210D $59 bytes: +$0B to +$63

@210F BPB_ZERO_LOOP
  Clears the bytes, then fills in the sector size and the cluster size.
  .210F One byte lower
  .2110 Cleared
  .2113 Keeps the BPB
  .2114 B = 2
  .2116 +$0C: bytes per sector $0200
  .2118 +$0D: sectors per cluster 1
  .211A Bits 0 and 2 of the media byte
  .211C Both give 4?
  .2120 No: 2 per cluster

@2121 BPB_MEDIA_ROOT
  One reserved sector, 2 FATs, $40 root entries, $70 when a cluster is 2 sectors.
  .2122 +$0E: 1 reserved sector
  .2125 +$10: 2 FATs
  .2127 +$11: $40 root entries
  .2129 1 sector per cluster?
  .212C No: $70 entries

@212E BPB_MEDIA_SIZE
  Works out the total sectors (HL), the sectors per track (D) and the sides (E): 8 or 9 per
  track from bit 1.
  .2130 Keeps +$13 (the total sectors)
  .2131 A = the media byte
  .2132 40 tracks x 8 sectors
  .2135 40 more for 9 sectors per track
  .2138 8 sectors per track, 1 side
  .213B 8 sectors per track (bit 1)?
  .213F No: 9 per track
  .2140 360 sectors

@2141 BPB_MEDIA_SIDES
  Bit 0 set: two sides, twice the sectors.
  .2141 Two sides?
  .2145 Yes: 2
  .2146 Twice the sectors

@2147 BPB_MEDIA_TRKS
  Bit 2 clear: 80 tracks, twice the sectors.
  .2147 40 tracks?
  .214B No: twice the sectors

@214C BPB_MEDIA_FAT
  Stores the total sectors and the media byte, and the sectors per FAT: 1 for $FA, $FE, $FF,
  3 for $F9, else 2.
  .214C DE = the total sectors
  .214D HL = +$13; the track geometry on the stack
  .214E +$13: the total sectors
  .2152 +$15: the media byte
  .2154 +$16: 1 sector per FAT
  .2155 $FE or $FF?
  .2157 Yes: 1
  .2159 $FA?
  .215B Yes: 1
  .215D 2
  .215E $F9?
  .2161 Yes: 3

@2162 BPB_MEDIA_GEOM
  Stores the sectors per track and the sides, and returns NC with HL = the BPB.
  .2164 D = sectors per track, E = sides
  .2165 +$18: sectors per track
  .2168 +$1A: sides
  .2169 HL = the buffer +$0B
  .216A NC

@216C FD_SET_PARAMS
  Takes the drive's parameters from the BPB at HL: the total sectors (IX+$09/$0D), the sectors
  per track and the sides. The record at +$50 (carry) gives 9 sectors per track and 2 sides
  when its +$0D byte is 2, else 1.
  .216C Keeps C: the record at +$50 was taken
  .2170 HL = BPB+$08: the total sectors
  .2175 The drive's total sectors, low
  .2178 ... and high
  .217B B = the media byte
  .217F C = sectors per track
  .2182 The record at +$50?
  .2185 A = the sides
  .2187 HL = BPB+$11: the hidden sectors

@218A FD_ALT_SIDES
  The record at +$50: 2 sides when its byte says 2, else 1.
  .218A 2 sides?
  .218E No: 1

@2190 FD_ALT_SPT
  The record at +$50: 9 sectors per track.
  .2190 9 sectors per track

@2192 FD_CHECK_SIDES
  Stores the sectors per track; a disk with as many sides as the drive's last one, or with one
  side, goes on; otherwise side 1 is checked (FD_SIDE1_TRY), H counting the tries.
  .2192 The drive's sectors per track
  .2195 The other set: B' = select bit, HL' = BPB
  .2196 Same sides as before?
  .219B One side?
  .219F H = the tries

@21A0 FD_SIDE1_TRY
  Reads an address field on side 1 of the current track. A time-out or change is mapped to its
  error; a record not found gives $96 (Incompatible disk); other failures are tried again.
  .21A0 Stay on the track; flags 0
  .21A3 Side 1
  .21A5 B = the drive's select bit
  .21A8 Reads an address field, side C
  .21AB Read: is it side 1?
  .21AD Time-out or disk change?
  .21AF Yes: map it
  .21B1 Record not found?
  .21B3 Yes: incompatible
  .21B5 Another try?

@21B8 FD_INCOMPAT
  Error $96 (Incompatible disk). $21BB, inside the LD HL, is LD A,$B4: the entry for error $B4
  (Not a DOS disk).
  .21B8 Error $96
  .21BA Hides LD A,$B4 at $21BB: error $B4 (Not a DOS disk)

@21BD FD_ERR_RET
  Returns the error in A with NZ.

@21BF FD_SIDE1_READ
  The field was read: its side byte (IY-$10) must be 1, else the disk is incompatible.
  .21BF The field's side
  .21C2 1?
  .21C3 No: incompatible
  .21C5 2 sides

@21C7 FD_SET_GEOM
  Stores the sides and the first sector's offset (the BPB's hidden sectors), then finds the
  last sector's track (LSN_TO_CHS): it must be 1-253. A media byte the drive had before keeps
  the flags; else a disk of fewer than 51 tracks clears bit 2, a larger one sets it and, unless
  bit 1 is set, has the double step checked (FD_CHECK_STEP).
  .21C7 The drive's sides
  .21CA HL = BPB+$11 again
  .21CB The hidden sectors
  .21CE The drive's first sector offset, low
  .21D1 ... and high
  .21D4 DE = the last sector
  .21D5 Its track, side and sector
  .21D8 Offset overflow: not a DOS disk
  .21DA Track above 255?
  .21DC Yes: NZ, A = the track's high byte
  .21DD Track 1-253?
  .21E1 No: not a DOS disk
  .21E3 The drive's last track
  .21E6 The media byte
  .21E7 The drive's media byte before?
  .21EA Yes: keep the flags
  .21EC 51 tracks or more?
  .21EF A = the drive's flags
  .21F2 Not over 50 tracks
  .21F4 Fewer: store them
  .21F6 Over 50 tracks
  .21F8 Double step known?
  .21FA Yes: store them
  .21FC C = the new flags
  .21FE B = the drive's select bit
  .2201 Checks the head steps on this disk
  .2205 C: incompatible ($96)

@2207 FD_CHECK_ERR
  A failed check is mapped to its error; else the flags worked out before the check (C) are
  stored, not those the check wrote.
  .2207 Failed: map it
  .2209 A = the new flags

@220A FD_STORE_FLAGS
  Stores the drive's flags and media byte.
  .220A The drive's flags
  .220D The drive's media byte

@2210 FD_PARAMS_OK
  Marks the parameters known and returns A = 0.
  .2210 Parameters known
  .2214 A = 0, Z

@2216 FD_ERR_MAP
  Turns DISK_IO's error bits in A into EXDOS error codes from the table at $2229, by the
  lowest set bit: 0 time-out $BA (Not ready), 1 mismatch $B9 (Verify error), 2 lost data and
  3 CRC $B8 (Data error), 4 not found $B7 (Sector not found), 5 disk changed $BF, 6 write
  protected $B6 (Write protected disk), 7 no drive or bad operation $BD. $BF also marks the
  drive's parameters not known. Exchanges the register sets (EXX) and returns NZ.
  .2216 One before the table

@2219 FD_ERR_FIND
  Steps through the table until the lowest set bit.
  .221A This bit set?
  .221D A = its error code
  .221E Swaps the register sets
  .221F Disk changed?
  .2223 Yes: parameters not known

@2227 FD_ERR_NZ
  Returns A with NZ.

@2231 FD_TRANSFER
  FLOPPY_DRIVER functions 4 (read), 5 (write) and 6 (write and verify): B sectors from logical
  sector DE, the caller's IX being the buffer.
  1. No count: A = 0. Parameters not known: A = $BF.
  2. The double step is checked when it was not yet (FD_CHECK_STEP).
  3. Refuses a run past the disk's end ($BB).
  4. Works a track (and side) at a time (FD_XFER_TRACK, FD_XFER_RETRY).
  It returns with the register sets exchanged, so the caller gets the counters kept in the
  alternates: B = the sectors done, C = those not done, HL = past the last one done. The flags
  do not always match A; DRIVER_CALL sets them with OR A.
  .2231 IX = drive's slot, A = count, B = select bit
  .2235 B' = 0: done
  .2237 C' = the count: left
  .2239 Parameters known?
  .223D No: error $BF
  .223F L = the count
  .2241 None: A = 0
  .2244 Step checked?
  .2248 No: check it now
  .224B C: incompatible ($96)
  .224E Failed: map it
  .2250 HL = the count
  .2251 Less 1
  .2252 HL = the last sector
  .2253 Past $FFFF
  .2256 BC = the disk's total sectors
  .225C The last sector inside the disk?
  .225F Yes: go

@2261 FD_PAST_END
  The run ends past the disk: error $BB (a code with no message). $2264, inside the LD HL, is
  LD A,$BF: the entry for parameters not known.
  .2261 Error $BB
  .2263 Hides LD A,$BF at $2264: parameters not known

@2266 FD_XFER_EXX
  Exchanges the register sets for the caller and returns.
  .2266 B = sectors done, C = not done, HL = buffer

@2268 FD_XFER_START
  The first sector's track, side and sector.
  .2268 DE = track, C = side, A = sector
  .226B D = the track
  .226C E = the sector

@226D FD_XFER_TRACK
  L = the sectors to do on this track and side: those left on it, or fewer when the count left
  (C') is smaller.
  .226D Sectors per track
  .2270 Less the first sector
  .2271 + 1: those left on the track
  .2272 C' = the count left
  .2273 More than that?
  .2276 No: all that is left

@2277 FD_XFER_PASS
  Transfers them (FD_XFER_RETRY); then goes on at sector 1 of the next side, or of the next
  track after the last side, until the count is done.
  .2277 L = sectors this pass
  .2279 Transfers them
  .227C Failed: map it
  .227E A = 0 OR the count left
  .2280 Done: A = 0 with the sets exchanged
  .2282 Sector 1
  .2284 Next side
  .2285 Past the last side?
  .2289 No
  .228B Side 0
  .228C Next track

@228D FD_XFER_NEXT
  The next pass.

@228F LSN_TO_CHS
  Turns a logical sector into its track, side and sector: adds the drive's first sector offset,
  divides by the sectors per track, and for two sides takes the low bit of the quotient as the
  side.
  Entry: DE = the logical sector; IX = the drive's slot.
  Exit: DE = the track; C = the side; A = the sector (from 1); NC.
  Error: C with A = 0 when the offset takes it past $FFFF.
  Changes: AF, C, DE, HL.
  Keeps: B, IX, IY.
  .228F A = 0
  .2290 HL = the drive's first sector offset
  .2296 Plus the sector
  .2298 Past $FFFF
  .2299 C = sectors per track
  .229C DE = the quotient, A = the sector - 1
  .229F C = the sides
  .22A2 NC: side 0 for one side
  .22A3 One side?
  .22A4 Yes
  .22A6 C = 0
  .22A7 DE = the track, carry = the side

@22AB LSN_SIDE_SECT
  C = the side from the carry, A = the sector from 1.
  .22AB C = the side
  .22AD The sector, from 1

@22AF DIV_DE_C
  Divides DE by C, bit by bit.
  Entry: DE = the dividend; C = the divisor (1-128).
  Exit: DE = the quotient; A = the remainder.
  Changes: AF, DE.
  Keeps: BC, HL, IX, IY.
  .22B0 16 bits
  .22B2 The remainder starts at 0

@22B3 DIV_DE_C_BIT
  Shifts the next bit into the remainder and subtracts the divisor when it fits.
  .22B3 Next bit of DE ...
  .22B7 ... into the remainder
  .22B8 Does the divisor fit?
  .22BB Yes: take it off
  .22BC Quotient bit 1

@22BD DIV_DE_C_NEXT
  The next bit.
  .22BF The caller's BC

@22DD ADDR_HERE
  Reads an address field on side 0 of the track the head is on (ADDR_READ with D = $FF).
  Entry: B = the drive's select bit; IY = the driver's variables.
  Exit: as ADDR_READ.
  Changes: AF, DE.
  Keeps: BC, HL, IX, IY.
  .22DD Stay on the track

@22DF ADDR_ON_TRACK
  Reads an address field on side 0 of track D, with no extra flags (ADDR_READ with E = 0).
  Entry: B = the drive's select bit; D = the track; IY = the driver's variables.
  Exit: as ADDR_READ.
  Changes: AF, E, D.
  Keeps: BC, HL, IX, IY.
  .22DF No extra flags

@22E1 ADDR_READ
  Reads one address field (DISK_IO 6) on side 0 into IY-$11 to IY-$0C: track, side, sector,
  size, CRC. $22E5 (inside LD A,$C5) is the entry that keeps the caller's C as the side.
  Entry: B = the drive's select bit; D = the track ($FF: stay); E = flags for DISK_IO's H (bit 5
  to the latch, bit 3 double step), added to the step rate in bits 0-1 of IY-$55; IY.
  Exit: A = D = DISK_IO's result (0 with Z when a field was read).
  Changes: AF, D; IY-$11 to IY-$0C.
  Keeps: BC, E, HL, IX, IY.
  .22E1 Keeps BC
  .22E2 Side 0
  .22E4 Hides PUSH BC at $22E5, the entry with C = the side
  .22E7 IX = IY, the caller's IX on the stack
  .22EC -$11
  .22EF IX = IY-$11: the field goes there
  .22F2 The step rate
  .22F7 With the flags
  .22F8 H = DISK_IO's flags
  .22F9 One field
  .22FB Read address
  .2300 The caller's IX
  .2304 D = the result

@2306 FD_XFER_ONCE
  One DISK_IO transfer for the function at IY-$12 (4 read, 5 write, 6 write then verify,
  8 write a track): double step when the drive's flags say so, write precompensation on tracks
  past half the last; then moves the counters on by the sectors done.
  Entry: B = the drive's select bit; C = the side; D = the track; E = the sector; L = the count;
  H = the sectors done so far; HL' = the buffer; B' = done, C' = left; IX = the drive's slot.
  Exit: A and the flags = DISK_IO's result (function 6: the verify's when it failed); E = the
  next sector; H = done so far + done now; L = the count less done now; HL' advanced 512 bytes a
  sector; B' + done, C' - done; E' = done now.
  Changes: AF, B, E, HL, AF', BC', E', H'.
  Keeps: C, D, IX, IY.
  .2306 Keeps the done and count
  .2307 The step rate
  .230C H = DISK_IO's flags
  .230D The drive's flags
  .2310 Double step, not over 50 tracks?
  .2316 Yes: double step

@2318 FD_XFER_PRECOMP
  Write precompensation past half the last track.
  .2318 The last track
  .231B Half of it
  .231C Track past that?
  .2320 Yes: precompensation

@2322 FD_XFER_OP
  Puts the buffer in IX and picks DISK_IO's operation: function 8 writes a track (4).
  .2323 The buffer ...
  .2324 ... in IX, the drive's slot on the stack
  .2327 The function
  .232A Format (8)?
  .232E Yes: 4, write a track

@2331 FD_XFER_RW
  Function 4 reads (1), functions 5 and 6 write (2).
  .2331 Function 4?
  .2333 Read
  .2337 Write

@2338 FD_XFER_CALL
  Runs the operation; for function 6 after sectors were written, verifies them (DISK_IO 3) and
  returns the verify's result when it failed, else the write's.
  .2338 Runs it
  .233B AF' = the result
  .233C The function
  .233F Write and verify?
  .2343 Any sectors written?
  .2346 Verify them
  .234B Verified: the write's result
  .234D The verify's result goes back

@234E FD_XFER_ADVANCE
  Moves the counters on by the sectors done (L) and returns DISK_IO's result.
  .234E IX = the drive's slot
  .2352 E = the next sector
  .2355 A = the sectors done
  .2357 E' = done now
  .2358 512 bytes a sector ...
  .235A ... H' = the buffer past them
  .235B B' = done in all
  .235E C' = left
  .2362 A = done now
  .2363 H = done so far, L = the count
  .2364 H = done in all
  .2366 L = the count ...
  .2368 ... less done now
  .236B A = DISK_IO's result

@236D FD_XFER_RETRY
  Transfers sectors with up to 4 tries' worth of retries (D'), each failure costing by kind: 5
  for a time-out, a disk change or write protection (no retry), 1 for a mismatch, lost data or
  CRC error, 2 for others. A first record not found reads an address field: a wrong track has
  the double step checked again. A retry goes on from the sector that failed.
  Entry: B = the drive's select bit; C = the side; D = the track; E = the first sector; L = the
  count; HL' = the buffer; B' and C' the counters (FD_XFER_ONCE); IX = the drive's slot;
  IY-$12 = the function.
  Exit: A = 0 with Z; L = the sectors done; E = the next sector; HL', B', C' moved on.
  Error: NZ with A = the error bits that ended it (masked by kind; $20 for a change found by the
  address read).
  Changes: AF, B, E, HL, AF', BC', DE', HL'.
  Keeps: C, D, IX, IY.
  .236E D' = the tries
  .2371 H = done so far

@2373 FD_XFER_LOOP
  One transfer; a failure is weighed by its bits.
  .2373 One transfer
  .2376 Done?
  .2377 Yes
  .2379 Keeps done and count
  .237A L = the error bits
  .237B Time-out, change, write protect?
  .237D Yes: no retry
  .2380 Mismatch, lost data, CRC?
  .2382 Yes: cost 1
  .2384 A = the tries left
  .2387 Retried before?
  .2389 A = the error bits
  .238A Yes: cost 2
  .238D Stay on the track, no flags
  .2390 Reads an address field, side C
  .2394 Read: right track?
  .2396 Disk change?
  .2398 Yes: no retry
  .239A No field: check the steps

@239C FD_RETRY_TRACK
  The head is on the right track: a retry at cost 2; on the wrong one the double step is
  checked again.
  .239C The track found
  .239F The one asked for?
  .23A0 Yes: retry

@23A2 FD_RETRY_RECHK
  Checks the double step again (FD_CHECK_STEP).
  .23A2 Checks the steps
  .23A5 Z when it worked

@23A6 FD_RETRY_SOFT
  Retry at cost 2 when the check worked, else no retry.
  .23A6 A = the error bits
  .23A7 Worked: cost 2

@23A9 FD_RETRY_COST
  H = the failure's cost (5 here; $23AC is LD H,1, $23AF LD H,2, entries inside the JPs, which
  never jump: Z is clear). Takes the cost off the tries (D'); while they do not go below 0,
  transfers again. Before that, a try other than the first calls DISK_IO with A = tries - 4
  ($FC-$FF), an operation it refuses with $80.
  .23A9 Cost 5: no retry
  .23AB Hides LD H,1 at $23AC: cost 1
  .23AE Hides LD H,2 at $23AF: cost 2
  .23B1 Keeps the error bits
  .23B3 A = the tries left
  .23B6 The first try?
  .23B8 No: DISK_IO refuses A = $FC-$FF
  .23BB A = the tries left
  .23BC Less the cost
  .23BE D' = the tries left
  .23C0 H = the error bits
  .23C1 A = them
  .23C2 H = done so far, L = the count
  .23C3 Tries left: again

@23C5 FD_XFER_END
  L = the sectors done; flags from A.
  .23C5 L = the sectors done
  .23C6 Z when A = 0

@23C8 FD_CHECK_STEP
  Checks how the head steps on this disk: forgets the track, seeks 8 steps and reads an address
  field (two tries). Track 8 found: single steps (for a disk of at most 50 tracks, bit 1 is
  cleared). Track 4: double steps (bit 2 cleared, bit 1 set). Track 16: error $96. Sets bit 0 of
  the drive's flags (checked), and forgets the track again at the end.
  Entry: B = the drive's select bit; IX = the drive's slot; IY.
  Exit: A = 0 with Z; bits 0-2 of IX-$07 set as above.
  Error: Z and C with A = $96 (Incompatible disk); NZ with DISK_IO's bits: $01/$20/$21 for a
  time-out or change, else the last read's result ($10 when another track was found).
  Changes: AF; IX-$07, the drive's track (forgotten).
  Keeps: BC, DE, HL, IX, IY.
  .23C8 Keeps BC
  .23CA Two tries
  .23CC Not checked
  .23D0 No double step

@23D4 STEP_CHK_TRY
  Forgets the track and reads an address field 8 steps in.
  .23D4 DISK_IO 0
  .23D5 Forgets the drive's track
  .23D8 Track 8, single steps
  .23DA Reads an address field there
  .23DD Read
  .23DF Time-out or disk change?
  .23E1 Yes: fail

@23E3 STEP_CHK_RETRY
  Another try, or fails with the last result in D.
  .23E3 Another try?
  .23E6 A = the result
  .23E7 NZ

@23EA STEP_CHK_FOUND
  Judges the track the field gives: 4 double steps, 8 single steps, 16 incompatible; another
  is tried again with D = $10.
  .23EA E = the flags ...
  .23ED ... marked double step
  .23EF The track found
  .23F2 4?
  .23F4 Yes: double steps
  .23F6 8?
  .23F8 Yes
  .23FA 16?
  .23FC D = $10, not found, for another
  .23FE Neither: again
  .2400 Error $96 (Incompatible disk)
  .2402 C

@2405 STEP_CHK_TRK8
  Track 8: single steps; bit 1 is kept for a disk over 50 tracks, else cleared.
  .2405 Over 50 tracks?
  .2407 Yes: keep bit 1
  .2409 No double step
  .240B Never jumps (Z); hides RES 2,E at $240C, the entry for track 4

@240E STEP_CHK_SET
  Marks the drive checked and stores the flags; A = 0.
  .240E Checked
  .2410 The drive's flags
  .2413 A = 0

@2414 STEP_CHK_END
  Forgets the drive's track and returns the result.
  .2414 Keeps the result
  .2415 DISK_IO 0
  .2416 Forgets the drive's track
  .241A The caller's DE and BC

@241D FD_FORMAT
  FLOPPY_DRIVER function 8: formats the disk for media byte B ($F8-$FF, else error $B4). Counts
  the drive's tracks (DISK_IO 8): an 80-track drive gives the disk over 50 tracks unless the
  media byte's bit 2 asks for 40 (then double steps); a 40-track drive forces bit 2 on. Then
  checks the drive answers on track 0 and builds the track image (FMT_BUILD_IMAGE).
  .241D IX = the drive's slot, A = the media byte
  .2420 C = the media byte
  .2421 $F8 or more?
  .2423 Error $B4 (Not a DOS disk)
  .2425 No: refused
  .2426 H = the step rate
  .2429 Count the drive's tracks
  .242E Failed: map it
  .2431 Flags: checked, double step
  .2433 An 80-track drive (L = $FF)?
  .2434 Yes
  .2436 40-track drive: single steps
  .2438 A 40-track media byte

@243A FMT_MEDIA_TRKS
  An 80-track disk (media bit 2 clear) is marked over 50 tracks.
  .243A A 40-track media byte?
  .243E No: over 50 tracks

@2440 FMT_BUILD_IMAGE
  Stores the drive's flags and reads an address field on track 0: a time-out, change or no drive
  gives $BA (Not ready). Then writes the track image into the buffer from the pairs at $2553
  (FILL_PAIRS): the track's header, then a sector at a time (FMT_IMG_SECTOR).
  .2440 The drive's flags
  .2443 Track 0 (A = 0 from DISK_IO)
  .2444 Reads an address field there
  .2447 Time-out, change, no drive?
  .2449 Error $BA (Not ready)
  .244C HL = the buffer
  .244D Kept
  .244E The track's header
  .2451 5 pairs
  .2456 9 sectors
  .2458 A' = sector 1

@245B FMT_IMG_SECTOR
  One sector's part of the image: the pairs at $255D for the address field (its track and side
  0, filled in later), the sector number, then 10 pairs for the size code 2, the gaps and 512
  bytes of $E5. After 9 sectors, the 2 pairs that follow give the track's end.
  .245B The sector's pairs
  .245E 4: up to the track and side
  .2463 A = the sector number
  .2464 Into the address field
  .2466 The next sector's number
  .2468 10 pairs: size, data field, gaps
  .246D Next sector
  .2470 The track's end: 2 pairs
  .2475 HL = the buffer
  .2476 Back to the caller's set

@24CD FMT_ALL_TRACKS
  One pass over every track of the disk for function 8: takes its return address into HL (the
  pass returns with JP (HL) at $2544), starts at track 0 and goes on at $24D0: per track and side
  8 or 9 sectors by the media byte, through FD_XFER_RETRY with the function at IY-$12 (8 writes
  the track image, its address fields given the track and side first).
  Entry: C = the media byte (bit 0 two sides, bit 1 8 sectors, bit 2 40 tracks); B = the drive's
  select bit; IX = the drive's slot; HL' = the track image; IY-$12 = the function.
  Exit: A = 0 when every track is done.
  Error: a failure leaves function 8 itself through FD_ERR_MAP; $C4B2 giving Z leaves it too.
  Changes: AF, C, DE, HL, AF', BC', DE', HL'.
  .24CD HL = the return address
  .24CE Track 0

@2545 FILL_PAIRS
  Writes B runs of bytes from a list of (count, byte) pairs; a count of 0 is 256.
  Entry: B = the pairs; DE = the list; HL = where to write.
  Exit: DE past the list; HL past the bytes; B = 0.
  Changes: A, B, DE, HL.
  Keeps: F, C, IX, IY.
  .2545 Keeps the pairs left
  .2547 B = the count
  .2549 A = the byte

question 2001 Function 0 jumps into DISK_IO 8 with IY 4 higher (the select-bit loop): does op 8 then work on the right variables?
question 2027 What does IY-$45 hold, which makes function 2 answer B = 1 rather than 0 for a drive with a change mark?
question 20BC Which disks carry the record at +$50 (byte +8 = 1, +$0D the sides) that BOOT_ALT_BPB takes?
question 22E1 What sets the step rate in bits 0-1 of IY-$55?
feedback wrong 23AB The card says the routine jumps out of the page to $0126 and $0226: both JP Z are never taken (NZ on every path in), they only hide LD H,1 and LD H,2
feedback wrong 240B The card says $2405 leaves for $93CB: JP NZ follows BIT 2,E with Z, so it never jumps; it hides RES 2,E at $240C
feedback missing 2216 The card does not say that $2216 reads the byte table at $2229-$2230 (indexed by the lowest set bit of A)
feedback missing 2231 No card says the functions 4-6 return with the register sets exchanged (EXX at $2266, $221E, $227E): the caller's B, C, HL are the counters kept in BC', HL'
feedback pattern 22C1-2545 The driver's per-drive arrays (IX = IY + drive: -$0B, -$07, -$03, +$01, +$05, +$09/+$0D, +$11/+$15, +$19) recur on 40 cards with no shared summary
feedback listing 1EE0-2000 Opened to see the dispatcher at $1FD3 (range 4's card) that enters every routine of this range
feedback listing 2440-2580 Opened for the format code past my cards ($2477-$2544) and the pair lists at $2553
feedback listing 1C9F-1DE2 Opened to learn what DISK_IO 7 returns in B and how DISK_IO treats IY
feedback wrong 236D The facts say C, IX and IY may change: C comes back from DISK_IO and the PUSH BC/POP BC around ADDR_READ and FD_CHECK_STEP, IX from POP IX at $234E, and nothing writes IY
feedback wrong 23C8 The facts say L may change: ADDR_READ pushes and pops HL around DISK_IO, and DISK_IO 0 does not touch L
